The `UserDetails` interface has four boolean methods beyond username and password. Each one can silently block authentication even when credentials are correct, and each throws a different `AuthenticationException` subclass:

- `isEnabled()` — `false` throws `DisabledException`. Use for soft-deleted or email-unverified accounts.
- `isAccountNonExpired()` — `false` throws `AccountExpiredException`. Use for time-limited accounts (contractors, trial users).
- `isAccountNonLocked()` — `false` throws `LockedException`. Use for brute-force lockout.
- `isCredentialsNonExpired()` — `false` throws `CredentialsExpiredException`. Use for mandatory password rotation policies.

By default, `DaoAuthenticationProvider` converts all four exceptions to a generic "Bad credentials" message — intentionally, to prevent user enumeration. This means a locked account looks identical to a wrong password from the outside, but from inside the logs you can distinguish them.

Implement these by mapping to real entity columns:

```java
public class AppUserDetails implements UserDetails {
    private final AppUser user;

    @Override
    public boolean isEnabled() { return user.isActive(); }

    @Override
    public boolean isAccountNonLocked() { return !user.isLocked(); }

    @Override
    public boolean isCredentialsNonExpired() {
        // null means no expiry; non-null means check the date
        return user.getPasswordExpiresAt() == null ||
               user.getPasswordExpiresAt().isAfter(Instant.now());
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isAccountNonExpired() { return true; } // adjust as needed
}
```

With this implementation, flipping `user.setLocked(true)` in the database immediately blocks the account on the next login attempt — no application code change needed.

The `loadUserByUsername` implementation loads the entity and maps it to `UserDetails`. The `ROLE_` prefix convention is the most common source of silent authorization failures:

```java
@Service
public class DatabaseUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    private final UserRepository userRepo;

    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {
        AppUser user = userRepo.findByEmail(username)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found: " + username));

        return new AppUserDetails(user); // custom class implementing UserDetails
    }
}
```

When building `GrantedAuthority` objects, prefix role names with `ROLE_`. Spring Security's `hasRole("ADMIN")` internally looks for `ROLE_ADMIN`. Without the prefix, role checks silently fail — the user authenticates successfully, but `hasRole()` returns false on every check because it looks for `ROLE_ADMIN` and finds only `ADMIN`.

```java
@Override
public Collection<? extends GrantedAuthority> getAuthorities() {
    return user.getRoles().stream()
        .map(role -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + role.getName()))
        .toList();
}
```

Spring Boot auto-detects your `UserDetailsService` bean and wires it into `DaoAuthenticationProvider` — no explicit configuration needed in most cases. If you define multiple `UserDetailsService` beans (for different authentication mechanisms), you must configure `DaoAuthenticationProvider` explicitly to specify which one to use.

For session-based applications, Spring Security calls `loadUserByUsername()` once at login and stores the result in the session. For stateless JWT applications, it's called only at token generation — subsequent requests don't touch the database. **The per-request database hit is only a concern for specific patterns**, like re-loading the user on every request to check current role assignments.

When caching is needed, Spring's `@Cacheable` works directly on `loadUserByUsername`:

```java
@Cacheable(value = "userDetails", key = "#username")
@Override
public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) {
    return userRepo.findByEmail(username)
        .map(AppUserDetails::new)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException(username));
}
```

Set a short TTL (1–5 minutes) and evict the cache entry when the user's roles or status change: `@CacheEvict(value = "userDetails", key = "#user.email")` on any update method.

**`UserDetailsPasswordService`** is the interface for transparent password algorithm upgrades. When a user logs in successfully and `DelegatingPasswordEncoder` detects the stored hash uses an outdated algorithm (e.g., BCrypt with cost factor 10 when you've moved to Argon2), Spring calls `updatePassword()` with the re-hashed value. You implement this to persist the new hash, and users are migrated lazily on login — no bulk migration, no forced password reset. Implement it on the same class as `UserDetailsService` and Spring wires it automatically.

`UserDetailsService` is the bridge between Spring Security and your user database. Its `loadUserByUsername()` method is the customization point for how Spring loads user details during authentication.

**The interface**
```java
@Service
public class CustomUserDetailsService implements UserDetailsService {
    @Override
    public UserDetails loadUserByUsername(String username) throws UsernameNotFoundException {
        return userRepo.findByEmail(username)
            .map(CustomUserDetails::new)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UsernameNotFoundException("User not found"));
    }
}
```

**`UserDetails` — what it represents**
- `getUsername()` and `getPassword()` — credentials
- `getAuthorities()` — list of `GrantedAuthority` (roles, permissions)
- `isAccountNonExpired()`, `isAccountNonLocked()`, `isCredentialsNonExpired()`, `isEnabled()` — four account state flags; all must return `true` for authentication to succeed

**Security considerations**
- Return the same error message for 'user not found' and 'wrong password' — different messages leak username existence to attackers (username enumeration)
- Do not log the password or the full `UserDetails` object

**Performance**
- With session-based authentication, `loadUserByUsername()` is called on every request to restore the `UserDetails`
- Cache with `@Cacheable` or use Spring Session with Redis to store the `UserDetails` in the session

Password hashing strength should match your hardware and threat model. The target: **~100ms per hash on your production hardware.** Fast enough that login latency is acceptable; slow enough that offline brute force is impractical. This target shifts as hardware improves — revisit the cost factor yearly.

For BCrypt, benchmark cost factors on your production-equivalent hardware:

```java
// Find the right cost factor for your hardware
for (int cost = 10; cost <= 16; cost++) {
    BCryptPasswordEncoder encoder = new BCryptPasswordEncoder(cost);
    long start = System.nanoTime();
    encoder.encode("benchmark-password");
    long elapsed = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;
    System.out.printf("Cost %d: %dms%n", cost, elapsed);
    if (elapsed >= 100) break; // found the right cost factor
}
```

For Argon2id, configure memory (64MB recommended), time (3 iterations recommended), and parallelism (2 threads) and benchmark similarly. Spring Boot's `Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8()` provides sensible starting defaults.

**DelegatingPasswordEncoder** for production use:

```java
@Bean
public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    // Argon2 for new passwords, accept BCrypt for existing
    Map<String, PasswordEncoder> encoders = new HashMap<>();
    encoders.put("bcrypt", new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12));
    encoders.put("argon2", Argon2PasswordEncoder.defaultsForSpringSecurity_v5_8());
    return new DelegatingPasswordEncoder("argon2", encoders);
}
```

With `UserDetailsPasswordService` implemented, users who log in with a BCrypt hash are automatically re-hashed with Argon2 and their hash is updated in the database — no user action, no bulk migration script, no downtime.
